the new june movie is a spectacular  cinematic experience that's vastly  different to david lynch's vividly weird  take on the original story by frank  herbert yippie kai movie lovers i'm jan  and in this video i'm explaining the  spiciest differences between denis  villeneuve's june and the 80s cult movie  lynch and veal nerve's films are worlds  apart when it comes to the villainous  characters of baron vladimir hakonen and  his nephew rabban aka the beast once  you've seen the 1984 version played with  maniacally ott gusto by kenneth mcmillan  who's covered in grotesque prosthetic  makeup and zooms through the air it's  not something you're ever likely to  forget his eye  then vladimir conan who encompasses his  doom stellen skarsgard gives a more  subtle less caricatured take on the  character making him memorable more for  his quietly menacing manner than his  gruesome cartoonish ways like the  original film even how he rises into the  air feels more ominous than the  exaggerated zoomy way it happens in  lynch's movie for veal nerve skarsgard's  eyes are the key to his frightening  performance as you feel the barons  always thinking tense and calculating  deep inside the first time we see him  he's hidden by steam giving the scene a  really atmospheric feel and reminding me  of the shadowy scenes of marlon brando's  colonel kurtz and apocalypse now as well  as the helmetless darth vader dave  bautista is back working with vel nerve  again after blade runner 2049 and  thankfully his interpretation of the  intimidating robin is much less  theatrical and sweaty than the 80s film  so far robin and his uncle play a  relatively small part in vielner's  cinematic vision of herbert's source  material more on that later as for the  baron's younger nephew fade there's no  sign of him at all at the moment i do  wonder if he'll pop up in part two  though and it would certainly be  interesting to see who veal nerve would  cast compared to the mad-eyed  over-acting of sting in lynch's film  despite june's dangerous dog-eat-dog  world there's one creature that seems  pretty much safe at all times and that's  the house of tradies pug whether sitting  patiently at home traveling through  space or venturing into battle the cute  royal canine always seems pretty chill  even if his presence in the film seems a  bizarre choice for a grand space opera  aiming to be star wars for grown-ups as  princess ireland actress virginia madsen  called it though we may never know  exactly why lynch made this weirdly  memorable and often beloved addition to  the world of june it may have something  to do with this particular breed's long  association with emperor's royalty and  the upper echelons of society from  ancient china to renaissance europe and  beyond  understandably though sadly for some  fans there are no pooches present in  vielner's version however the director  has made his own strange new addition  with a creepy giant spider creature with  uncanny humanoid features such as hands  at the ends of its legs the harkonnen  pet is listed in the movie's credits as  a human spider proxy and the creature's  performer is melena siddharova a  choreographer who came up with the  famous spider dance you might have seen  on youtube the spider creature appears  to understand human communication as it  responds to the benny jesuit reverend  mother when she orders it to leave the  room and this has led many fans to  speculate that the harkonnens turned dr  ue's wife into the spider it's a  plausible theory given that in the  original source material her fate was  never known although spoiler warning now  for character fates in june 2021 going  forward the baron implies that uh's wife  was dead at the time he kills the doctor  i said i'd set her free that you could  join her so join her the character of  duncan idaho played by jason momoa is  hugely improved in vlnerv's version  getting more screen time and his  relationship with paul is better  developed than in lynch's movie where  the two characters share just a couple  of scenes veal nervous described his  duncan as a kind of big brother  character who paul idolizes momoa's  duncan also gets an epic fight scene  where he sacrifices himself to take down  a slew sardikar warriors to buy time for  paul jessica and kynes to escape and  it's a pretty badass moment when he  pulls a sword out of his chest and takes  out another bunch of baddies before  eventually keeling over duncan's death  in lynch's movie is much more  anti-climactic with a projectile slowly  penetrating his defensive shield and  those holtzman energy shields were one  of the oddest things in the 80s movie  with the roblox like polygon shapes  surrounding a character making scenes  such as gurney and paul's fight training  just a mass of blurry forms facing off  them piling on top of each other  thankfully special effects have  massively improved since then and the  personal shields in 2021's movie are  much smoother and less flurry so you can  still see the characters clearly enough  kyle mclaughlin didn't do a bad job as  paul given the material he had to work  with especially all those endless  internal monologues he and several of  the other actors were forced to bring to  life however timothy chalamet's pool and  his past becoming the super being known  as a quizatz hadorak is much better  developed first of all although chalamet  was in his early 20s when filming paul  is around 15 to 16 in the original book  and the actor plays the part of a  somewhat inexperienced teenager thrust  into a deadly world of political  maneuverings much more convincingly than  in lynch's movie considering that  originally it's supposed to be a  coming-of-age story lynch's paul feels  much more like the man in his twenties  that the actor was at the time and too  self-assured also the way that velner  foreshadows paul's connection to arrakis  is teased more elegantly for starters  paul's visions of june and his future  there are clearer more nuanced and  detailed than lynch's more cryptic  clunky visions also veal nerve has added  more depth to scenes such as a sandworm  attack on the crawler in the new movie  as paul gets out of the ornithopter he  becomes entranced by the desert while  hearing mysterious voices chanting in  lynch's film paul never gets off the  ship and instead simply gets a little  intoxicated by the smell of the spice  the men bring in with them spice pure  unrefined  spice the look of vielner's worms is  also quite different and the director  explained to empire magazine that it  took over a year to come up with every  little detail that would make such a  beast possible from the texture of its  skin to the way the mouth opens to the  system it uses to eat its food in the  sand and to find the perfect shape that  looked prehistoric enough overall the  new sand worms seem even larger than  lynch's version which was more closely  based on the original designs by the  book's illustrator with its distinctive  three-lobed mouth  something we didn't get much of in the  2021 movie was worm riding with only a  small moment at the end where paul sees  a freman riding a worm in the distance  there's your power in lynch's film paul  memorably rides a worm and the creatures  are used as part of the fremen  resistance and attacks on the harkonnen  occupiers because this happens in the  latter part of the story it's safe to  say that we can look forward to timothy  chalamet riding a giant worm in the  sequel while lynch's film opens with the  emperor's daughter introducing us to the  world field nerve gives charney the job  of briefly being our guide to arrakis  given her prominent placing in the  movie's marketing you'd be forgiven for  thinking that zendaya must have quite a  substantial role but that's not the case  in fact her screen time consists mostly  of brief glimpses in paul's visions  until near the end of the film where she  and paul finally meet and she gets a bit  of real dialogue indeed her words are  the final ones we hear in the movie this  is only the beginning although zendaya  spent only four days filming for part  one in part two her character will have  a much larger more important place in  the story which i'm looking forward to  as she's a talented actress as for sean  young though lynch does cover the whole  book she's still only a very small part  of a much bigger movie and doesn't get  any especially interesting dialogue to  craft a truly memorable part with  when it comes to paul's mother lady  jessica velner has built up her role  compared to francesca anisa's version  giving her more time training and  defending her son rebecca ferguson is  perfectly cast bringing the delicate mix  of power vulnerability and mystery  that's required for her character whose  role encompasses parent partner and  member of the formidable and influential  benny jesuit who have the commanding  power of the voice which doubtless  inspired the jedi mind tricks of star  wars another character who gets more  screen time in the new movie is dr kinds  and compared to max von siddow in  lynch's movie sharon duncan brewster  goes out heroically exacting revenge on  the colonisers ravaging her planet i  serve only one master his name is  sometimes lynch's june feels more like a  collective hallucination or weird fever  dream that i had rather than a film i  actually watched it's frequent wacky  visuals and garish aesthetic certainly  give it a very distinctive look which  has its fans the latest film's look  however is wildly different more david  lean specifically lawrence varabia than  david lynch with touches of apocalypse  now vilnerv's own arrival and  cinematographer greg fraser's rogue one  there's plenty of lavish sweeping vistas  and arresting visuals but the director  never loses a sense of what really  matters his characters and the film's  muted more natural looking tones make  its pops of color stand out but in a  grounded more realistic way  trying to pack the whole of frank  herbert's expansive sci-fi novel into a  single film did not work out well for  david lynch the movie ended up heavy on  melodrama and overloaded with plot and  masses of expositionary dialogue not  only did it perform poorly at the box  office but it also was critically mauled  with reviewers calling it  incomprehensible stupefyingly dull and  disorderly and the worst movie of the  year even lynch himself disowned the  film and viewed it as a failure citing  his lack of artistic freedom making him  at the opposite end of the spectrum  denivil nerve says he's never felt such  deep joy or gratitude watching one of  his movies as he has done with june  vilnerv actually refused to adapt the  novel unless the story was split over  more than one film as the world was too  complex so the new movie brings to life  roughly half the book building its world  and introducing its concepts characters  cultures and planets though this does  mean the ending's a bit less satisfying  than you might like the sequel will  cover the rest while for vl nerve part 1  is like an appetizer part 2 is the main  meal an insane playground that will  allow him to go berserk and really  create and as if that's not enough the  director has even said that he'd like to  make a third film adapting the second  book in the june saga messiah i love the  overall epic feel of vielner's doom but  what do you think and if you've seen  both movies which do you prefer let me  know in the comments below and if you  enjoyed this then a thumbs up and a  share are hugely appreciated you can tap  here to discover all the epic easter  eggs and details you might have missed  in squid game or tap right for something  else you're sure to like thanks for  watching and see you next time yippee  kai movie lovers

